Z||000000||squid||09-03-99||05:17 PM||||Who makes high rate springs for fiero's?
Z||000001||88 Fiero||09-03-99||05:22 PM||||currently...if you want a custom rate on a spring...you have to go and buy coils over...there are alot of companies that sells coils over with the spring rate you want...examples like RCC, Carrera and Ryane Motors.....
Z||000002||VISCERAL||09-04-99||09:12 AM||||CHECK OUT THESE SITES ALSO FOR SUSPENSION STUFF.<br>http://www.ground-control.com/default.htm<p>http://www.shelbycustom.com/f-coil/f-coil.html
Z||000003||theogre||09-04-99||05:01 PM||||have you rebushed the system in urethane yet? I'd do that before replacing springs. The rebush really stiffens things allot all by itself.
Z||000004||lowCG||09-04-99||05:38 PM||||The Ground Control kits would work in the rear,then you could get springs from a number of places,like H&R,Eibach,Betz,or many others.In the front,you'd probably need to create a better,stronger mount for a coilover unit,or buy(make)some new,tubular control arms,however,there are other,easier ways to achieve the same net results without spending all that money,and being mostly a hobby/passion,I chose to play around in the shop,and come up with something effective.
Z||000005||88 Fiero||09-05-99||12:49 AM||||you also have to be careful with hight rate springs...first you want to find out for what kind of driving conditions are you planning with your car...if you are doing 1/4 mile race that's fine....it's not really recommended in streets..unless you have a good chiropractor...also on high rate springs  on fiero's when your driving fast and hit a bump...expect the car to be airborne...and be sure you have a good insurance coverage....
Z||000006||Cooter||09-05-99||02:06 PM||||Question??? On my 4 cyl Fiero, the front springs seem stiff enough, but the rears are soft feeling. The front of the car stays planted, but the rear has a lot of bounce (new shocks,struts and rear sway bar). Would the springs from my v6 parts car stiffen up the rear? Or are they the same spring rate? The v6 car was a GT, mine is a SE. I dont want to make the car higher in the back than it already is, but just make it a little more firm. Any suggestons other than Fiero store lowering springs- I aint made of money!
Z||000007||88 Fiero||09-05-99||07:20 PM||||Cooter...what yr. is your 4 cylinder and the GT V6 spring?....if the yr. of both cars is the same or '84 to '87 you can use it...The Fiero GT uses the WS6 Sport Suspension which is a little more firmer suspension springs...You can cut the spring about an inch so it would drop it down a bit...what kind of shocks do you use?...I have a friend with an '85 GT..He lowered the car..cut the springs and uses Koni's adjustable...and have it set to the softest settings...his ride now even tho' the spring got firmer co'z he cut it..was really nice...
Z||000008||Cooter||09-05-99||07:23 PM||||My car is an 85 and the parts car is 86. The struts are Monroe sensatrac-cheap! I have to put new cv joint boots on soon, so I will probably swap the springs then. Thanks.
